From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 02:11:23 +0200
Subject: arm64: dts: sophgo: add Milk-V Duo S pin header overlays

The arm64 half of the previous patch: the same eight overlays for the 26-pin
header, and the same Makefile arrangement, under arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/.

The .dtso files are byte-identical to the RISC-V ones and could not usefully be
shared: overlays are built per architecture from the directory next to the base
DTS they patch, and the arm64 build never descends into arch/riscv. What makes
identical sources correct on both sides is that the entire SoC description is
shared - arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000.dtsi includes cv180x.dtsi and
cv181x.dtsi straight out of arch/riscv with SOC_PERIPHERAL_IRQ() redefined - so
&pinctrl, &i2c1, &spi3, &uart1, &uart2, &pwm0 and &pwm1 are the same nodes with
the same labels, and the pad and function numbers come from the same
dt-bindings header either way.

Keep the two copies in step. A change to one that is not made to the other
gives the two architectures different header pinouts on the same board, which
is exactly the drift the single shared patch series exists to prevent.

Verified the same way: every overlay applies to the arm64 base with fdtoverlay,
alone and as a set.

Signed-off-by: Armbian <info@armbian.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/Makefile                        | 28 ++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c1.dtso        | 66 ++++++++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c4.dtso        | 45 +++++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm0.dtso        | 55 ++++++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm1.dtso        | 45 +++++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3-spidev.dtso | 38 ++++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3.dtso        | 51 +++++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart1.dtso       | 64 +++++++++
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart2.dtso       | 48 +++++++
 9 files changed, 440 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/Makefile b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/Makefile
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/Makefile
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/Makefile
@@ -18,3 +18,31 @@ dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-c906l.dtbo
 sg2000-milkv-duo-s-usb-device-dtbs := sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dtb overlay/sg2000-usb-device.dtbo
 dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += sg2000-milkv-duo-s-usb-device.dtb
 dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-usb-device.dtbo
+
+# Pin header overlays: the 26-pin header (J3) has i2c1, i2c4, spi3, uart1,
+# uart2 and two PWM chips reachable on it, and the board DTS routes pads to
+# none of them. Each of these turns one of those on, selectable through
+# armbianEnv.txt.
+#
+# One composite target covers all of them rather than one apiece. fdtoverlay
+# applies them to the base in sequence, so a single build checks that every one
+# still resolves against it, and through base-dtb-y in scripts/Makefile.dtbs it
+# is what makes the base DTB get -@ - without those symbols U-Boot's "fdt apply"
+# cannot resolve &pinctrl at boot. The result is never booted, which is just as
+# well: several of these overlays want the same pads, so applying the whole set
+# produces a tree that contradicts itself. dtb-y is what gives the base its -@,
+# so it is installed too; only the individual .dtbo files are of use at boot.
+sg2000-milkv-duo-s-header-dtbs := sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dtb \
+	overlay/sg2000-i2c1.dtbo overlay/sg2000-i2c4.dtbo \
+	overlay/sg2000-spi3.dtbo overlay/sg2000-spi3-spidev.dtbo \
+	overlay/sg2000-uart1.dtbo overlay/sg2000-uart2.dtbo \
+	overlay/sg2000-pwm0.dtbo overlay/sg2000-pwm1.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += sg2000-milkv-duo-s-header.dtb
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-i2c1.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-i2c4.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-spi3.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-spi3-spidev.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-uart1.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-uart2.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-pwm0.dtbo
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_SOPHGO) += overlay/sg2000-pwm1.dtbo
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c1.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c1.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c1.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: I2C1 on the 26-pin header (J3).
+ *
+ *     pin 11   IIC1_SDA   XGPIOB[11]   VIVO_D10, function 6
+ *     pin 13   IIC1_SCL   XGPIOB[12]   VIVO_D9,  function 6
+ *
+ * Enable with
+ *
+ *     overlays=i2c1
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt. The i2c1 controller is already enabled in the board
+ * device tree; nothing routes pads to it, which is why it does nothing until
+ * this overlay - or Milk-V's duo-pinmux - is used.
+ *
+ * These are not the pins Milk-V's published pinout labels I2C1. That labels
+ * pin 7 (XGPIOB[18], IIC1_SCL on function 5) and pin 9 (XGPIOB[17], IIC1_SDA
+ * on function 5), and pin 9 does not exist as a GPIO on current boards: it is
+ * XGPIOB[17] on hardware v1.1 and plain GND from v1.2 on. Nothing else on this
+ * header can carry IIC1_SDA, so the vendor pairing cannot be reproduced on a
+ * board anyone is buying today. Pins 11 and 13 can, and both are ordinary
+ * header pins on every revision.
+ *
+ * bias-pull-up because the Duo S has no pull-ups on these two pins - they are
+ * general-purpose header pins, not a dedicated bus - and an I2C bus with none
+ * at all does not come up. The internal ones are weak, about 60k, which is
+ * enough for a short lead at 100kHz and not much more. Fit real ones on the
+ * bus if it misbehaves; they simply parallel these.
+ *
+ * bias-pull-down = <0> is not redundant with that. These pads come up with the
+ * pull-down already enabled - conf reads 0x48 on every pad this overlay set
+ * touches - and cv1800_pconf_compute_config() puts only the properties a group
+ * actually names into its write mask, which cv1800_set_pinconf_config() then
+ * applies as a read-modify-write. So bias-pull-up on its own leaves both pulls
+ * on: 60k up against 62k down, which parks a released line near half rail, well
+ * short of the ~2.3V a 3.3V bus needs to read as a high. Naming the pull-down is
+ * what clears it. Check it rather than trusting it - conf on VIVO_D10 and
+ * VIVO_D9 in /sys/kernel/debug/pinctrl/3001000.pinctrl/pins reads 0x44 when this
+ * worked and 0x4c when it did not.
+ *
+ * Conflicts with the pwm0 overlay, which wants the same two pads for PWM_1 and
+ * PWM_2.
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/pinctrl-sg2000.h>
+
+&pinctrl {
+	i2c1_cfg: i2c1-cfg {
+		i2c1-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D10, 6)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D9, 6)>;
+			bias-pull-up;
+			bias-pull-down = <0>;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&i2c1 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c1_cfg>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c4.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c4.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-i2c4.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: I2C4 on the 26-pin header (J3).
+ *
+ *     pin 3    IIC4_SCL   XGPIOB[20]   VIVO_D1, function 7
+ *     pin 5    IIC4_SDA   XGPIOB[21]   VIVO_D0, function 7
+ *
+ * Enable with
+ *
+ *     overlays=i2c4
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt.
+ *
+ * These are the pins Milk-V's pinout labels I2C4, and the vendor bootloader
+ * already routes them: cvi_board_init() does PINMUX_CONFIG(VIVO_D1, IIC4_SCL)
+ * and PINMUX_CONFIG(VIVO_D0, IIC4_SDA), commented "I2C4 for TP" - the bus a
+ * touch panel would hang off. So this bus works today without any overlay, on
+ * inherited pin state. This overlay exists so that it stops depending on which
+ * bootloader ran, the same argument the eMMC pad group in the board DTS makes.
+ *
+ * No bias-pull-up here, unlike the i2c1 overlay: the vendor routes this bus for
+ * a panel that brings its own pull-ups, and the board is laid out for that.
+ * Add them in a local copy if your device does not.
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/pinctrl-sg2000.h>
+
+&pinctrl {
+	i2c4_cfg: i2c4-cfg {
+		i2c4-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D1, 7)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D0, 7)>;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&i2c4 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c4_cfg>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm0.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm0.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm0.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: the pwm0 chip's outputs on the 26-pin header (J3).
+ *
+ *     pin 11   PWM_1   XGPIOB[11]   VIVO_D10, function 0   -> &pwm0 1
+ *     pin 13   PWM_2   XGPIOB[12]   VIVO_D9,  function 0   -> &pwm0 2
+ *     pin 19   PWM_3   XGPIOB[13]   VIVO_D8,  function 0   -> &pwm0 3
+ *
+ * Enable with
+ *
+ *     overlays=pwm0
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt. One pwmchip appears with four channels, of which
+ * channel 0 goes nowhere: PWM_0 is not brought out on either header.
+ *
+ * All three pins at once, not one overlay per pin, because a device tree node
+ * has exactly one "default" pinctrl state and the pinctrl core applies only
+ * that one. Two overlays each setting pinctrl-0 on &pwm0 would not compose -
+ * the second would silently replace the first, and one of the two pins would
+ * never be muxed. Per-chip is the finest grain that works. Take a local copy if
+ * you want a subset.
+ *
+ * Function 0 is the pads' reset function for pins 11 and 13, so those two come
+ * out of the BootROM as PWM already; declaring them here is what stops that
+ * from being an accident. Pin 19 defaults to VI2_D_8.
+ *
+ * There are alternates, if these three collide with something else you want:
+ * PWM_2 is also on pin 12 (function 6) and PWM_3 also on pin 3 (function 6).
+ * Swapping to those frees pins 13 and 19 for i2c1 and spi3 respectively, at the
+ * cost of pins 12 and 3, and needs a local copy of this file.
+ *
+ * Conflicts as shipped with i2c1 (pins 11, 13) and spi3 (pin 19).
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/pinctrl-sg2000.h>
+
+&pinctrl {
+	pwm0_cfg: pwm0-cfg {
+		pwm0-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D10, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D9, 0)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D8, 0)>;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&pwm0 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&pwm0_cfg>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm1.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm1.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-pwm1.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: the pwm1 chip's outputs on the 26-pin header (J3).
+ *
+ *     pin 22   PWM_6   XGPIOA[18]   JTAG_CPU_TCK, function 2   -> &pwm1 2
+ *     pin 18   PWM_7   XGPIOA[19]   JTAG_CPU_TMS, function 2   -> &pwm1 3
+ *
+ * Enable with
+ *
+ *     overlays=pwm1
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt. Four channels appear and two of them go nowhere:
+ * PWM_4 and PWM_5, channels 0 and 1, are on pins 8 and 10, which carry the boot
+ * console. This overlay leaves them alone. Wiring them up means giving up the
+ * serial console, so it belongs in a local copy and not in a shipped default.
+ *
+ * See the pwm0 overlay for why the channels of one chip are muxed together
+ * rather than one overlay per pin.
+ *
+ * power-source = <1800> on a 3.3V header is deliberate; the uart1 overlay,
+ * which shares these two pads, explains it.
+ *
+ * Conflicts with the uart1 overlay, which wants both of these pads.
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/pinctrl-sg2000.h>
+
+&pinctrl {
+	pwm1_cfg: pwm1-cfg {
+		pwm1-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_JTAG_CPU_TCK, 2)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_JTAG_CPU_TMS, 2)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&pwm1 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&pwm1_cfg>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3-spidev.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3-spidev.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3-spidev.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: a spidev node on SPI3 chip select 0.
+ *
+ * Enable alongside the overlay that routes the pads:
+ *
+ *     overlays=spi3 spi3-spidev
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt. On its own this one gives you /dev/spidev3.0 with no
+ * pins behind it, which is not useful; kept separate because a board with a
+ * real in-kernel driver on this bus wants spi3 without a spidev squatting on
+ * the chip select.
+ *
+ * "rohm,dh2228fv" is not what is on the bus. spidev refuses to bind to a node
+ * whose compatible is the bare string "spidev" - spidev_probe() warns and fails
+ * - so every board that wants a userspace SPI device borrows the compatible of
+ * some real part from spidev's own table. This is the one the rest of Armbian
+ * borrows; treat it as a keyword, not a claim about the hardware.
+ *
+ * 1 MHz is a deliberately unambitious default that works on a breadboard lead.
+ * spi-max-frequency is a ceiling, not a rate: the ioctl can ask for less, never
+ * more, so raise it here if the device on the other end can take it.
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+&spi3 {
+	#address-cells = <1>;
+	#size-cells = <0>;
+
+	spidev3_0: spidev@0 {
+		compatible = "rohm,dh2228fv";
+		reg = <0>;
+		spi-max-frequency = <1000000>;
+		status = "okay";
+	};
+};
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-spi3.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: SPI3 on the 26-pin header (J3).
+ *
+ *     pin 19   SPI3_SDO    XGPIOB[13]   VIVO_D8, function 5   (MOSI)
+ *     pin 21   SPI3_SDI    XGPIOB[14]   VIVO_D7, function 5   (MISO)
+ *     pin 23   SPI3_SCK    XGPIOB[15]   VIVO_D6, function 5
+ *     pin 24   SPI3_CS_X   XGPIOB[16]   VIVO_D5, function 5   (CS0)
+ *
+ * Enable with
+ *
+ *     overlays=spi3
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt. This routes the bus and nothing else - a bus with no
+ * devices on it registers and then sits there. Add a device as a child of &spi3
+ * in an overlay of your own, or use spi3-spidev for the userspace interface.
+ *
+ * As with i2c4, the vendor bootloader already routes these four pads and the
+ * bus therefore works today on inherited pin state; the overlay is what makes
+ * that independent of which bootloader ran.
+ *
+ * CS is the controller's own chip select on one line, so this bus addresses one
+ * device. num-cs stays at the driver default. For more, drive further selects
+ * as GPIOs with cs-gpios, which needs its own pinmux for whichever header pins
+ * you pick.
+ *
+ * Conflicts with the pwm0 overlay, which wants pin 19 for PWM_3.
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/pinctrl-sg2000.h>
+
+&pinctrl {
+	spi3_cfg: spi3-cfg {
+		spi3-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D8, 5)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D7, 5)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D6, 5)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D5, 5)>;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&spi3 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&spi3_cfg>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart1.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart1.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart1.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: UART1 on the 26-pin header (J3).
+ *
+ *     pin 18   UART1_TX   XGPIOA[19]   JTAG_CPU_TMS, function 6
+ *     pin 22   UART1_RX   XGPIOA[18]   JTAG_CPU_TCK, function 6
+ *
+ * Enable with
+ *
+ *     overlays=uart1
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt, for a second serial port at 3.3V on those two pins.
+ *
+ * It should come up as /dev/ttyS1, but do not hardcode that: the board DTS
+ * aliases serial0 to uart0 and nothing else, so every other port takes the
+ * lowest free 8250 line number in probe order rather than one fixed by the
+ * device tree. Match on the device path if it matters.
+ *
+ * These pads are the CPU JTAG lines at reset, and the vendor bootloader has
+ * already given up on that: cvi_board_init() muxes both to plain GPIO, and
+ * Milk-V's own pinout hands them to a touch panel's interrupt and reset. So
+ * taking them for a second serial port costs nothing that was not already
+ * spent, and it is the only UART1 pairing on this header that does not collide
+ * with the console. UART1_TX and UART1_RX are also on pins 8 and 10 (function
+ * 4), which is where UART0 - the boot console - lives.
+ *
+ * No flow control. UART1_RTS is on pin 18 (function 4) and UART1_CTS on pin 22
+ * (function 4), i.e. the same two pads as TX and RX here; one pad cannot carry
+ * both, and nothing else on J3 reaches them.
+ *
+ * power-source = <1800> and the header is 3.3V, which looks wrong and is
+ * deliberate. Both pads are in the VDDIO_EMMC domain, which the board DTS
+ * already declares as 1800 for the eMMC group, and cv1800_set_power_cfg()
+ * rejects a second, different value for a domain it has already seen - the
+ * whole pin map fails, and the consumer never probes. The value feeds the
+ * drive-strength and schmitt translation in pinconf and writes no voltage
+ * register, and this group sets neither, so it is inert here. What VDDIO_EMMC
+ * actually is on this board has never been measured; until it has been, keeping
+ * every group in the domain on one value is the only safe option.
+ *
+ * Conflicts with the pwm1 overlay, which wants the same two pads for PWM_7 and
+ * PWM_6.
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/pinctrl-sg2000.h>
+
+&pinctrl {
+	uart1_cfg: uart1-cfg {
+		uart1-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_JTAG_CPU_TMS, 6)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_JTAG_CPU_TCK, 6)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&uart1 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&uart1_cfg>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart2.dtso b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart2.dtso
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sophgo/overlay/sg2000-uart2.dtso
@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Milk-V Duo S: UART2 on the 26-pin header (J3).
+ *
+ *     pin 12   UART2_TX   XGPIOB[19]   VIVO_D2,  function 7
+ *     pin 15   UART2_RX   XGPIOB[22]   VIVO_CLK, function 7
+ *
+ * Enable with
+ *
+ *     overlays=uart2
+ *
+ * in /boot/armbianEnv.txt, for a third serial port on those two pins. It should
+ * come up as /dev/ttyS2; see the uart1 overlay for why that is probe order and
+ * not a promise.
+ *
+ * Note which pads these are *not*. The pads named UART2_RX/TX/CTS/RTS carry
+ * Bluetooth on this board - cvi_board_init() routes all four to uart4, which is
+ * the AIC8800's HCI - and the board DTS says so in a comment. Routing uart2
+ * there takes Bluetooth down with it. UART2 is reachable from the header only
+ * as an alternate function of other pads, which is what this overlay uses, and
+ * the two here are chosen because nothing else on J3 needs them: TX is also on
+ * pins 11, 23 and 26, RX also on pins 13 and 24, all of which the i2c1, spi3
+ * and pwm0 overlays want.
+ *
+ * No flow control: UART2_CTS and UART2_RTS reach only pins 9 and 7, and pin 9
+ * is GND on hardware v1.2 and later.
+ */
+
+/dts-v1/;
+/plugin/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/pinctrl-sg2000.h>
+
+&pinctrl {
+	uart2_cfg: uart2-cfg {
+		uart2-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_D2, 7)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_VIVO_CLK, 7)>;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&uart2 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&uart2_cfg>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
-- 
Armbian

